---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../../lib/siteUrl';
---

<Layout
  title="Depth Blur — Varve Docs"
  description="How to generate, import, reuse, and edit Varve's relative depth maps for blur and non-destructive masks."
>
  <div class="container-custom">
    <article class="doc-page">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href={sitePath('/docs')}>Docs</a> / Depth Blur
      </nav>
      <h1>Depth Blur and Depth Masks</h1>
      <p class="lead">Use one reusable relative DepthMap for focus blur, range masks, and localized edits while keeping the source image editable.</p>

      <h2>Generate a DepthMap</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Select an image-filled shape and open <strong>Depth Blur</strong> in the Inspector.</li>
        <li>Choose <strong>Enable Depth Blur</strong> to download the optional local model, then choose <strong>Generate Depth Map</strong>.</li>
        <li>Turn on <strong>Preview Depth</strong> to inspect the near-to-far field. Use <strong>Pick Focus</strong> to sample a focal depth from that preview.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Adjust and save the effect</h2>
      <p>
        Tune blur amount, focal distance, and transition range. Saving creates a <code>depthBlur</code>
        effect that references the document's DepthMap resource. It does not replace the image with a
        blurred bitmap, so the same map can later drive additional depth-aware effects. The Inspector
        preview and saved replay use the same canonical depth-aware compositor.
      </p>

      <h2>Use the same map as a mask</h2>
      <p>
        Open <strong>Adjustments → Depth Mask</strong> on the image, or select an Adjustment Layer and
        choose its source image. Pick a generated map, reuse a saved map, or import Varve's
        self-describing <code>.vdepth.json</code> resource. The contained preview, valid-sample
        histogram, legend, and <strong>Sample near</strong>/<strong>Sample far</strong> controls make
        the map's orientation visible before you edit the range.
      </p>
      <p>
        Set numeric near/far endpoints and separate depth-domain transitions. Choose
        <strong>Replace</strong>, <strong>Intersect</strong>, <strong>Add / union</strong>, or
        <strong>Subtract</strong> when a raster mask already exists, then choose
        <strong>Apply depth mask</strong>. This does not enable blur or change source pixels. The
        resulting mask stays in the existing Mask surface, where the Refine Mask brush can add,
        subtract, and protect coverage without rerunning inference.
      </p>

      <h2>Revisit, remove, and work offline</h2>
      <p>
        The accepted map, range recipe, and resolved mask coverage are saved separately from the
        blur effect. Changing a range is a lightweight edit; it does not rerun the model. Removing
        <strong>Depth Blur</strong> leaves a map that is still referenced by a mask. Reopening,
        refining, and exporting an accepted mask work without the model being installed. Use
        <strong>Regenerate Depth Map</strong> only when the source image or estimate needs to change.
      </p>

      <h2>Persistence and invalidation</h2>
      <p>
        A persisted map records its dimensions, normalized near/far convention, model and preprocessing
        provenance, source asset hash, and a versioned 16-bit scalar payload. Rendering only needs the
        stored resource; model inference is needed only to create or regenerate it. If the source asset
        changes, Varve marks the saved map stale and asks you to regenerate it.
      </p>

      <h2>Limitations</h2>
      <p>
        Generated depth is relative, not metric, and is a guide for image effects rather than a
        measurement or 3D reconstruction. Transparent objects, reflections, hair, wires, thin
        structures, and severe occlusion may need manual coverage refinement or a separate matte.
        The current scalar import is Varve's validated <code>.vdepth.json</code> profile; arbitrary
        grayscale PNG, EXR, HEIC, and camera-depth metadata are not silently treated as depth.
        Missing or corrupt maps fail soft and preserve the last resolved coverage where available.
      </p>

      <p><a href={sitePath('/features/depth-aware-effects')}>Read the depth-aware effects overview →</a></p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .doc-page { max-width: 760px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 3rem 0 5rem; }
  .breadcrumb { font-size: 0.875rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
  .breadcrumb a { color: var(--text-secondary); }
  h1 { font-family: var(--font-editorial); font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 2.75rem); color: var(--text-primary); }
  .lead { font-size: 1.2rem; color: var(--text-secondary); margin-bottom: 2.5rem; }
  h2 { margin-top: 2.5rem; color: var(--text-primary); }
  p, li { color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.75; }
  li + li { margin-top: 0.65rem; }
  code { color: var(--text-primary); }
</style>
